module Core

import Native

{-
   The block form of `pushHex` - sixteen bytes read, thirty-two units written, and no table anywhere.

   `Format/Hex.yana` beside this states what the operation is and why it is lower case; what is here
   is the half the target decides.

   **The digit is arithmetic and not a lookup, and that is forced rather than chosen.** A nibble to
   its digit is the classic `pshufb` of a sixteen-entry table, and `shuffle` in this language takes
   a *compile-time* pattern (Design-Vector §3.3) - the pattern is a field of the instruction and is
   read back out of the IR, so there is no operation here whose index vector is computed at run time
   and a nibble lookup is not expressible. What replaces it is `d + 48` with a further `7` where `d`
   is above nine, which is one compare and one blend per vector - the same expression `hexUnit`
   writes as a branch, and cheaper than the load a table would have been anyway.

   **Sixteen lanes, written down, for `interleaveLow`'s reason** - the same one `Core/Read.yana`'s
   digit scan states: the interleave is one instruction at sixteen lanes on every x86 back to SSE2
   and there is none at thirty-two below `avx512`, because every AVX2 shuffle works inside its own
   128-bit half. So a build at `avx2`, where `Vec(U8)` means thirty-two lanes, would not compile this.
   Two `punpck`s turn the high and low nibble vectors into the thirty-two output units in their final
   order. At `avx512` the interleave at thirty-two lanes is `vpermi2b`, and `Hex.native.avx512.yana`
   replaces this body with the same loop at that width - 1.7x, test/bench/avx512/findings.md §5.

   The write goes into the string's own run rather than through `pushUnit`, which is what the
   reservation is for: the units are provably in range once the capacity has been checked, so the
   two stores are stores. `String.appendUnits` makes the same check for the same reason - a reserve
   the allocator refused is a write past the end of the run and not a lost append.
-}
pub fn pushHex(bytes: [U8], &to: String):
    let n = length(bytes)
    if n <= 0 then return

    String.reserve(to, n + n)

    let start = to.units.length :: Size
    let wanted = start + n + n
    checkCondition((to.units.run.capacity :: Size) < wanted)
    to.units.length = truncate(wanted) :: Count

    let source = bytes.items
    let target = to.units.run.items + start

    let nibble = splat(15) :: Vec(U8, 16)
    let nine = splat(9) :: Vec(U8, 16)
    let digit = splat("0" :: U8) :: Vec(U8, 16)
    let letter = splat(("a" - 10) :: U8) :: Vec(U8, 16)

    let &i = 0 :: Size
    while i + 16 <= n:
        let block = vectorAt(source + i) :: Vec(U8, 16)
        let high = (block `shr` (splat(4) :: Vec(U8, 16))) `and` nibble
        let low = block `and` nibble
        let highText = high + select(high .> nine, letter, digit)
        let lowText = low + select(low .> nine, letter, digit)

        let low16 = bitcast(interleaveLow(highText, lowText)) :: Vec(CodeUnit, 16)
        let high16 = bitcast(interleaveHigh(highText, lowText)) :: Vec(CodeUnit, 16)
        setVectorAt(target + i + i, low16)
        setVectorAt(target + i + i + 16, high16)
        i = i + 16

    -- The ragged end, one byte at a time, because there is no overreading *store*: a write past the
    -- run is the destruction of whatever follows it, where a read past it is §5.2's padding.
    while i < n:
        let byte = bytes[i] :: Int
        store(target + i + i, hexUnit(byte `shr` 4))
        store(target + i + i + 1, hexUnit(byte `and` 15))
        i = i + 1
